## Removal Considerations

### Services Safe to Remove

#### System-Level Monitoring Services (High Priority for Removal)
1. **thermal-monitor.service** - Can be removed if argo_lifecycle_manager.py is updated to read thermal zones directly
   - Installed: `/usr/local/bin/thermal-monitor.sh`, `/etc/systemd/system/thermal-monitor.service`
   - Status: enabled & active
   
2. **memory-monitor.service** (Shell-based) - Redundant with psutil-based monitoring
   - Installed: `/usr/local/bin/memory-monitor.sh`, `/etc/systemd/system/memory-monitor.service`
   - Status: enabled & active
   - Note: Different from Python memory_monitor.py development tool
   
3. **cursor-monitor.service** - Development/debugging tool, not critical for Argo operations
   - Installed: `/usr/local/bin/cursor-monitor.sh`, `/etc/systemd/system/cursor-monitor.service`
   - Status: enabled & active
   
4. **orangepi-monitor.service** - Potentially redundant with other monitoring
   - Installed: `/etc/systemd/system/orangepi-monitor.service`
   - Status: enabled & active
   - Uses native `orangepimonitor` command

#### Application-Level Monitoring (Medium Priority for Removal)
5. **argo-storage-monitor.service** - Can be removed if GUI notifications are not needed
   - Installed: User service (orangepi)
   
6. **memory_monitor.py** (Python) - Development tool for ROS2 node leak detection, safe to remove anytime
   - Location: `launch/memory_monitor.py`
   - Not a systemd service (manual execution)
   
7. **argo_service_monitor.py** - Can be removed if recording status monitoring is not needed
   - Location: `launch/argo_service_monitor.py`
   
8. **argo_file_monitor.py** - Development tool, safe to remove anytime
   - Location: `launch/argo_file_monitor.py`

### Services to Keep
1. **temp_monitor.py (ROS2 node)** - Core temperature monitoring with alerts, should remain
   - Provides real-time temperature data to ROS2 topics
   - Essential for autonomous operation safety
   
2. **storage_monitor.py** - CLI tool for manual storage checks, useful to keep
   - Lightweight, on-demand usage only

### Migration Path for Removal

#### Thermal Monitoring Removal
1. Update argo_lifecycle_manager.py to read thermal zones directly instead of log files
2. Remove systemd service installation
3. Delete thermal-monitor.sh and thermal-monitor.service

#### Storage Notification Removal  
1. Ensure CLI storage_monitor.py is sufficient
2. Remove argo-storage-monitor.service
3. Optionally keep argo_storage_notifications.py for manual use

#### Memory Monitor Cleanup
1. Archive any saved CSV files
2. Delete memory_monitor.py if no longer needed for development

## Quick Reference Commands

### Service Status Checks
```bash
# Check all monitoring services at once
sudo systemctl status thermal-monitor memory-monitor cursor-monitor orangepi-monitor
systemctl --user status argo-storage-monitor

# Check individual services
sudo systemctl status thermal-monitor.service
sudo systemctl status memory-monitor.service
sudo systemctl status cursor-monitor.service
sudo systemctl status orangepi-monitor.service

# View service logs
tail -f /var/log.hdd/persistent/thermal-$(date +%Y%m%d).log
tail -f /var/log.hdd/persistent/memory-$(date +%Y%m%d).log
tail -f /var/log.hdd/persistent/processes-$(date +%Y%m%d).log
tail -f /var/log.hdd/persistent/cursor-processes-$(date +%Y%m%d).log
tail -f /var/log.hdd/persistent/orangepi-monitor-$(date +%Y%m%d).log
```

### Manual Monitoring Tools
```bash
# Manual storage check
python3 launch/storage_monitor.py

# ROS2 node memory leak detection (30 min, 10s interval)
nohup python3 launch/memory_monitor.py > memory_monitor.log 2>&1 &
```

### Uninstall Services

#### System-Level Services (in /usr/local/bin and /etc/systemd/system)
```bash
# Thermal monitor
sudo systemctl stop thermal-monitor.service
sudo systemctl disable thermal-monitor.service
sudo rm /etc/systemd/system/thermal-monitor.service
sudo rm /usr/local/bin/thermal-monitor.sh
sudo systemctl daemon-reload

# Memory monitor (shell-based)
sudo systemctl stop memory-monitor.service
sudo systemctl disable memory-monitor.service
sudo rm /etc/systemd/system/memory-monitor.service
sudo rm /usr/local/bin/memory-monitor.sh
sudo systemctl daemon-reload

# Cursor monitor
sudo systemctl stop cursor-monitor.service
sudo systemctl disable cursor-monitor.service
sudo rm /etc/systemd/system/cursor-monitor.service
sudo rm /usr/local/bin/cursor-monitor.sh
sudo systemctl daemon-reload

# Orange Pi monitor
sudo systemctl stop orangepi-monitor.service
sudo systemctl disable orangepi-monitor.service
sudo rm /etc/systemd/system/orangepi-monitor.service
sudo systemctl daemon-reload
```

#### User-Level Services
```bash
# Storage monitor (GUI notifications)
systemctl --user stop argo-storage-monitor.service
systemctl --user disable argo-storage-monitor.service
systemctl --user daemon-reload
```

### Complete Monitoring Service Cleanup
```bash
# One-liner to remove all non-essential monitoring services
sudo systemctl stop thermal-monitor memory-monitor cursor-monitor orangepi-monitor
sudo systemctl disable thermal-monitor memory-monitor cursor-monitor orangepi-monitor
sudo rm /etc/systemd/system/{thermal-monitor,memory-monitor,cursor-monitor,orangepi-monitor}.service
sudo rm /usr/local/bin/{thermal-monitor,memory-monitor,cursor-monitor}.sh
systemctl --user stop argo-storage-monitor
systemctl --user disable argo-storage-monitor
sudo systemctl daemon-reload
systemctl --user daemon-reload
```
